From 9db14caa22ff70352699aa3ec4c8ee76e89df21d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 09:48:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Install Julia with circuitscaper's own downloader, not
 JuliaCall's

JuliaCall's install_julia() in the current CRAN release (0.17.6)
installs Julia 1.9 when asked for the latest version and can't install
any Julia >= 1.10, and a fixed release is blocked on an unreachable
maintainer. The rest of CRAN JuliaCall works fine, so cs_install_julia()
now downloads Julia itself:

- Reads Julia's official release index (versions.json), picks the newest
  stable release by version (not string) comparison, and the portable
  archive for R's own platform and architecture (.tar.gz on Linux glibc
  and macOS, not musl or the .dmg; .zip on Windows).
- Verifies the download's size and, on R >= 4.5, its SHA-256 checksum,
  unpacks into a staging directory, and only then replaces any earlier
  circuitscaper-installed Julia under tools::R_user_dir("circuitscaper").
- find_julia_bin() looks there right after JULIA_HOME and before
  JuliaCall's install record, so cs_install_julia(force = TRUE) replaces
  a stale JuliaCall-installed Julia 1.9.
- cs_setup() passes the Julia it found to julia_setup() explicitly, so
  the version-checked Julia is the one that starts, and stops clearly if
  an older Julia is already running in the session.
- Drops the CRAN-JuliaCall detection and the "install JuliaCall from
  GitHub" advice. Adds rjson (already a JuliaCall dependency) and tools
  to Imports, withr to Suggests.
- Tests install a fake Julia from a local release index; a new CI job
  runs cs_install_julia() from scratch with CRAN JuliaCall on Linux,
  macOS, and Windows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
